---
description: Dependency lifetimes and construction
globs: ["libs/*/src/**/*.ts", "libs/*/src/**/*.tsx", "apps/api/src/**/*.ts"]
alwaysApply: true
---

Three layers. Do not blur them.

1. Primitives (`SqlClient`, `Io`) are the only long-lived dependencies. Thread them
   through request context. Never build a container/registry object.

2. Use-case classes take primitives in the constructor: `(db: SqlClient, io: Io)`.
   One class per endpoint/use case. Write methods open `db.transaction`. Read
   methods construct Layer C with the request `db` as `SqlTx` without a write
   transaction. Use cases must not call other use cases.

3. Repositories, services, publishers, and handlers take `SqlTx` (never
   `SqlClient`) plus `Io`. `SqlTx` is `Pick<SqlClient, 'query' | 'run'>` from
   `@pkgs/sql-client/interface`. Construct them inline inside a
   `db.transaction(async (tx) => …)` callback (writes) or at the point of use
   (reads). Never store one on a field of anything longer-lived than that
   callback. Never construct one eagerly "in case it's needed."

If wiring repeats across use cases, extract a factory FUNCTION taking `(tx, io)` —
never a class that holds constructed objects.

Read `now` per-operation via `io.clock.now()`; don't capture it at construction.

Do not convert `defineMutation` / `defineQuery` `run` handlers, React hooks, or
`wallIo()` composition roots. Inside mutations, `new XRepository(tx)` is correct.

CI: `ci:no-top-level-di-functions`, `ci:no-eager-di-construction`,
`ci:layer-c-sql-tx`, `ci:no-di-container`.

Full rationale: docs/dependency-injection.md
